\section{Introduction (with Literature Review)}

\subsection{Background}

Randomized Controlled Trials (RCTs) remain the gold standard for evaluating the efficacy and safety of novel therapeutic interventions. 
However, the clinical development sector is increasingly constrained by rising operational costs, strict time limitations, and complex 
ethical considerations \citep{dimasi_innovation_2016}. For instance, recruiting a sufficient number of patients, particularly in case of 
rare diseases, poses a major obstacle to the development of new treatments. Furthermore, randomizing patients to a placebo or active 
control arm when other effective treatments already exist raises significant ethical concerns regarding patient welfare \citep{freedman_scientific_1987, world_medical_association_world_2025}.

Historically, the dominant paradigm in the design of RCTs has been the frequentist approach. This framework evaluates long-run operating 
characteristics, relying heavily on strict Type I Error control. A fundamental limitation of this paradigm is that it treats every trial 
in isolation, ignoring pre-existing information such as data from previous clinical phases or historical control arms that could otherwise 
help the current study. 

Conversely, Bayesian statistical frameworks offer a mathematically rigorous solution to this challenge by incorporating external evidence 
through informative prior distributions. Integrating background knowledge during the planning phase can lead to highly efficient designs, 
accelerating trial execution, reducing operational waste, and limiting the number of concurrent patients required for the control arm 
(\note{TODO, in Stallard et al. there are nice citations of papers that uses this}). 
This not only decreases the overall sample size but also makes the trial more attractive to potential participants, who face a lower 
probability of being assigned to a placebo group. As noted by O'Hagan et al., \textit{"the design of a trial 
is ultimately the sponsor’s decision and its outcome is the sponsor’s risk; therefore, the design should incorporate all available information to 
maximize the probability of a satisfactory outcome"} \citep{ohagan_assurance_2005}.
Recognizing these practical benefits, regulatory bodies are increasingly supportive of these methods, as 
evidenced by recent guidance on the use of Bayesian methodology in clinical trials \citep{noauthor_use_nodate}.



\subsection{Bayesian Designs and Operating Characteristics}
The Bayesian framework formally incorporates uncertainty into the design through prior distributions, that can be placed either directly 
on the treatment effect or on one (or both) arms:

\begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Borrowing on the Control Group:} when recruiting patients for a standard-of-care or placebo arm is ethically challenging or 
    operationally slow, historical data can be used to inform the control parameters. Designs that supplement a smaller concurrent control group 
    with historical information are formally referred to in the literature as hybrid-control trials. \note{TODO, in Weru et a. there are examples of papers
    that use hybrid control trials, cite them} 


    \item \textbf{Borrowing on the Treatment Effect:} When the primary goal is to use existing efficacy data to support a new indication or 
    demographic, such as leveraging adult data to pediatric cohorts, the prior can be placed on the treatment contrast directly \citep{psioda_bayesian_2019}.
\end{itemize}




Evaluating the operating characteristics of Bayesian designs requires particular attention. Traditional frequentist designs rely on 
conditional power, estimating the probability of success assuming a single, fixed treatment effect. The Bayesian framework shifts instead the focus 
to unconditional power, or Assurance, which evaluates the expected power averaged over a prior distribution for the unknown treatment effect.
Integrating over a prior provides a much more realistic assessment of the predictive probability of achieving a 
statistically significant result \citep{spiegelhalter1993bayesian, ohagan_assurance_2005}. Alternative names are probability of study success (Wang et al., 2013) 
and Bayesian predictive power (Spiegelhalter, Freedman and Blackburn, 1986). 

Despite these fundamentally different philosophies regarding power, it remains common practice to evaluate Bayesian designs with informative priors 
using the classical, frequentist Type I Error rate. However, when Type I Error is defined traditionally, conditioning solely on the sampling distribution 
of the current trial's data, it is mathematically impossible to strictly control it if external information is being borrowed 
\citep{viele_use_2014, psioda_bayesian_2019, kopp-schneider_power_2020}, and a consensus on alternative metrics has yet to be reached in the literature. 


\subsection{Information Borrowing and Prior-Data Conflict \note{TO REFINE}}
\label{subsec:info-borrowing}

While borrowing historical data presents clear advantages, it is not without risk. It is possible that historical data and current trial 
data are heterogeneous due to shifts in demographics, standard of care, or measurement techniques \citep{evans_checking_2006}. If a model 
forces the integration of historical and concurrent data when such a discrepancy exists, it can lead to severely biased estimates and inflated error rates. 
Indeed, the naive use of historical data leads to unacceptable Type I Error inflation under prior-data 
conflict.

\note{TO DO: Schmidli (2015) has a nice literature review about all possible borrowing methods, add it here. } 
Recognizing these dangers, early frequentist guidelines emphasized strict criteria to ensure external data were 
sufficiently comparable before any pooling \citep{pocock_combination_1976}. Modern Bayesian frameworks address the risk of prior-data conflict through dynamic 
borrowing. Rather than blindly pooling or entirely discarding historical data, dynamic borrowing algorithms assess the congruency between the historical evidence and the accumulating new data.
 If the historical data aligns well with the current trial, the model "borrows" strength from it, increasing precision. Conversely, if there is a 
 true difference between the historical and current parameters, commonly referred to as "drift" (\note{TO DO: find citations of who called it "drift" first}), the model dynamically discounts the historical 
 information, relying more on the observed data.

Several approaches have been proposed to achieve this dynamic behavior. Examples include Power Priors \citep{ibrahim_power_2000}, 
Commensurate Priors \citep{hobbs_2011}, and Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) priors \citep{noauthor_summarizing_nodate}. This thesis will focus
 primarily on the MAP framework, and specifically on its robust extension. Standard MAP summarizes historical information into a predictive prior, but it can sometimes be too slow to react to prior-data conflict, 
 Robust Mixture Priors have therefore been proposed \citep{schmidli_robust_2014}. By blending an informative MAP component with a diffuse robust component, 
 these priors provide safer results in presence of drift, ensuring robust inference even under prior misspecification (\citep{richeldi2022trial}, \citep{chen2024efficacy}). 


 
