Reference to the average t1e metric propose by Psioda and Ibrahim. which averages the conditional power over the null design prior, (restricted tot the null trat effect)
Best point that when you borrow information on a treatment contrast, the prior information usually supports a positive, non-null treatment effect. 
This creates a "fundamental inconsistency" between the analysis prior (which favors efficacy) and the null design prior (which assumes no efficacy). 
Because of this inconsistency, you cannot achieve the same strong, guaranteed control of the average Type I Error that you get when borrowing on the control arm

Furthermore, they note that if you try to strictly control the average Type I Error in this scenario, you are essentially forced to discard the historical prior information.
This metric takes the average Type I Error and multiplies it by the prior probability that the treatment effect is actually null or harmful.

If you want you can compute it anyway, otw, focus on the proposed better alternative metric for this scenario: pre-posterior probability of actually drawing a false positive conclusion (or Type III error).



\subsection{The Experimental Unit Information Index}
\label{subsec:euii}


Now we will extend the Experimental Unit Information 
Index (EUII), recently introduced by \cite{held2025balancing}, to 
the Bayesian borrowing framework. 

\paragraph{The Diagnostic Odds Ratio}
The Diagnostic Odds Ratio (DOR) is a summary of a binary test's discriminatory performance borrowed 
from the diagnostic accuracy literature \citep{glas2003diagnostic}. For a 
statistical hypothesis test with Type I Error rate $\alpha$ and 
power $1 - \beta$, the DOR is defined as the ratio of the odds 
of rejection under the alternative to the odds of rejection under 
the null:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_dor}
    \text{DOR} 
    = \frac{(1-\beta)/\beta}{\alpha/(1-\alpha)}
    = \frac{\text{Power}/(1-\text{Power})}
           {\text{T1E}/(1-\text{T1E})}.
\end{equation}
%
A $\text{DOR} > 1$ indicates that the odds of a positive 
decision are higher under the alternative than under the null, 
which holds for any test with power exceeding its Type I Error 
rate. The DOR increases with power and decreases with Type I 
error inflation.

The classical EUII of \cite{held2025balancing} normalizes the DOR
by the sample size $n_{\text{trial}}$ to obtain a per-unit measure of
evidentiary efficiency:
%
\begin{equation} \label{eq:classical_euii}
    \text{EUII} = \text{DOR}^{1/n_{\text{trial}}}.
\end{equation}
%
This can be interpreted as the geometric mean contribution of a 
single experimental unit to the total discriminatory evidence of 
the design. A value $\text{EUII} > 1$ indicates that each 
additional unit increases the odds of correctly distinguishing 
the alternative from the null on average.

\subsubsection{Borrowing on the control}
 To quantify the per-patient information 
yield, we define two variants of the Experimental Unit Information Index (EUII). 

The \textbf{Conditional EUII ($\text{EUII}_{cond}$)} uses classical conditional metrics: 
$$DOR_{cond}(\theta_C^*, \delta) = \frac{\text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta) / (1 - \text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta))}{\text{T1E}(\theta_C^*) / (1 - \text{T1E}(\theta_C^*))}$$

Normalizing this ratio by the sample size $n_{\text{trial}}$ yields the Conditional EUII:
$$\text{EUII}_{cond}(\theta_C^*, \delta) = \left[ DOR_{cond}(\theta_C^*, \delta) \right]^{\frac{1}{n_{\text{trial}}}}$$ 

It tells us exactly how much information each patient provides if the true control value is exactly $\theta_C^*$.
 
 
The \textbf{Averaged EUII ($\text{EUII}_{avg}$)} 
By using average Power and T1E, we obtain the Averaged DOR:
$$DOR_{avg}(\delta) = \frac{\text{avgPower}(\delta) / (1 - \text{avgPower}(\delta))}{\text{avgT1E} / (1 - \text{avgT1E})}$$

Normalizing this yields the Averaged EUII:
$$\text{EUII}_{avg}(\delta) = \left[ DOR_{avg}(\delta) \right]^{\frac{1}{n_{\text{trial}}}}$$

Remark: It is important to note that $DOR_{avg}(\delta)$ is not equivalent to the design-prior expectation of the pointwise DOR:
$$DOR_{avg}(\delta) \neq \int DOR_{cond}(\theta_C, \delta) \pi_D(\theta_C) d\theta_C$$
because the ratio of expectations does not, in general, equal the expectation of a ratio. 


\paragraph{Which sample size to use}

The definition in Equation~\eqref{eq:classical_euii} leaves open a question that is crucial under Bayesian borrowing: what is $n$?

The robust MAP analysis prior blends an informative MAP component with a vague component, controlled by a robustness weight $\omega \in [0, 1]$: at $\omega = 0$ the prior reduces to the full MAP prior and at $\omega = 1$ it becomes entirely vague. The effective sample size $n_{\text{ESS}}$ of the analysis prior therefore depends on $\omega$: it is largest for the full MAP ($\omega = 0$) and shrinks to zero for the vague prior ($\omega = 1$). One might therefore argue for a penalized denominator $n_{\text{eff}} = n_{\text{trial}} + n_{\text{ESS}}$, attributing part of the evidentiary gain to the historical patients.

We argue that this is the wrong normalisation. The EUII is a design tool: it answers how much evidence each newly enrolled patient contributes. Historical patients are already given; they have been collected and encoded in the prior before the current trial begins. Including $n_{\text{ESS}}$ in the denominator conflates two separate decisions: the prior choice (which $\omega$ to use) and the trial design (how many new patients to enroll). This conflation is particularly misleading because a more informative prior (low $\omega$, high $n_{\text{ESS}}$) would be penalised in the denominator for the very information it provides, while a vague prior ($\omega = 1$, $n_{\text{ESS}} = 0$) would not. The denominator should reflect the quantity the investigator actually controls.

We therefore use throughout:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:euii_avg}
    \text{EUII} = \left[ \text{DOR}_{\text{avg}} \right]^{1 / n_{\text{trial}}}
\end{equation}
where $n_{\text{trial}}$ is the number of patients enrolled in the current trial, which is the decision variable of the trial design.

\subsubsection{Borrowing on treatment \note{TO DO}}




\subsection{Group Sequential Design with One Interim Analysis}
\label{subsec:sequential}

A group sequential design (GSD) with one interim analysis extends the fixed design by allowing the trial to stop early at a pre-specified interim look, either for \emph{efficacy} (overwhelming evidence of treatment benefit) or for \emph{futility} (insufficient evidence to justify continuing). Early stopping reduces the expected number of patients required when the signal is clear, but introduces dependence between interim and final decisions that must be accounted for when evaluating operating characteristics.

\subsubsection{Design Setup}
\label{subsubsec:seq_setup}

Let $K = 2$ denote the total number of stages. Patients are recruited in two cohorts; the cumulative sample sizes for arm $m \in \{T, C\}$ at stage $k \in \{1,2\}$ are $n_{m,1} < n_{m,2}$, with $\Delta n_{m,2} = n_{m,2} - n_{m,1}$ additional patients enrolled between stages. The total planned sample size is $n_{\text{trial}} = n_{T,2} + n_{C,2}$, reached only if the trial runs to the final analysis.

At each stage $k$, the sufficient statistics are the cumulative sample means $\bar{y}_{T,k}$ and $\bar{y}_{C,k}$. Under normal sampling with known variances:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,k} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m,\; \frac{\sigma_m^2}{n_{m,k}}\right).
\end{equation*}

The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ and the vague treatment prior are updated at each stage using the cumulative arm means, yielding the stage-$k$ posterior for the treatment contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$:
\begin{equation*}
  \delta \mid \bar{y}_{C,k}, \bar{y}_{T,k} \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat{\delta}_k, V_{\delta,k}),
\end{equation*}
where $\hat{\delta}_k$ and $V_{\delta,k}$ follow from the Normal-Normal conjugacy derivations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}.

\paragraph{Stopping rules at the interim}
After the stage-1 data have been collected, two Bayesian criteria are evaluated simultaneously:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Efficacy:} the trial stops early for success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta > q \mid \bar{y}_{C,1}, \bar{y}_{T,1}) > p.
  \end{equation*}
  \item \textbf{Futility:} the trial stops early without success if
  \begin{equation*}
    \Pr(\delta > q' \mid \bar{y}_{C,1}, \bar{y}_{T,1}) < p_{\text{fut}},
  \end{equation*}
  for a (possibly identical) threshold $q'$ and a futility level $p_{\text{fut}}$.
\end{itemize}
If neither rule triggers, the trial continues to stage 2 where only the efficacy criterion is re-evaluated. Every trial trajectory therefore terminates in one of four mutually exclusive outcomes: (i) efficacy stop at stage 1, (ii) futility stop at stage 1, (iii) success at stage 2, or (iv) failure at stage 2.

A key consequence of Bayesian borrowing is that the decision boundary is not fixed on a $Z$-score scale. Because the posterior at stage $k$ depends on both arm means through the analysis prior on the control, the critical treatment mean that triggers a decision is a \emph{function} of the observed control mean $\bar{y}_{C,k}$. This data-adaptive boundary is the reason why operating characteristics cannot be derived from the Brownian motion representation used in classical frequentist GSD theory \citep{jennison_group_1999}, and numerical integration over the joint distribution of arm means is required instead.

\subsubsection{Conditional Operating Characteristics}
\label{subsubsec:cond_seq}

For fixed true parameters $(\theta_C^*, \delta)$, the conditional operating characteristics are computed by numerical integration over the joint distribution of the cumulative arm means at each stage. Since normal sampling has independent increments, the interim means $(\bar{y}_{T,1}, \bar{y}_{C,1})$ and the new-cohort means at stage 2 are mutually independent, so the cumulative mean at stage 2 satisfies:
\begin{equation*}
  \bar{y}_{m,2} = \frac{n_{m,1}\,\bar{y}_{m,1} + \Delta n_{m,2}\,\bar{y}_{m,\Delta 2}}{n_{m,2}}, \qquad \bar{y}_{m,\Delta 2} \mid \theta_m \sim \mathcal{N}\!\left(\theta_m, \frac{\sigma_m^2}{\Delta n_{m,2}}\right).
\end{equation*}

Integrating over the marginal distribution of $(\bar{y}_{T,1}, \bar{y}_{C,1})$ at stage 1, and over the conditional distribution of $(\bar{y}_{T,2}, \bar{y}_{C,2})$ for trials that do not stop early, yields the conditional stopping probabilities:
\begin{align*}
  P_{\text{eff},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{efficacy stop at stage 1} \mid \theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  P_{\text{fut},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{futility stop at stage 1} \mid \theta_C^*, \delta), \\
  P_{\text{cont}}(\theta_C^*, \delta)   &= 1 - P_{\text{eff},1} - P_{\text{fut},1}, \\
  P_{\text{suc},2}(\theta_C^*, \delta)  &= \Pr(\text{efficacy at stage 2} \mid \text{continue}, \theta_C^*, \delta) \cdot P_{\text{cont}}.
\end{align*}

The overall conditional Power and Type I Error are then:
\begin{align}
  \text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta) &= P_{\text{eff},1}(\theta_C^*, \delta) + P_{\text{suc},2}(\theta_C^*, \delta), \label{eq:seq_power} \\
  \text{T1E}(\theta_C^*)            &= \text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta = 0). \label{eq:seq_t1e}
\end{align}

The conditional expected sample sizes, overall and conditional on the trial outcome, are:
\begin{align}
  E[N \mid \theta_C^*, \delta]   &= n_1\!\cdot\!(P_{\text{eff},1} + P_{\text{fut},1}) + n_2\!\cdot\! P_{\text{cont}}, \label{eq:seq_en} \\
  E[N^+ \mid \theta_C^*, \delta] &= \frac{n_1 \cdot P_{\text{eff},1} + n_2 \cdot P_{\text{suc},2}}{\text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta)}, \label{eq:seq_en_plus} \\
  E[N^- \mid \theta_C^*, \delta] &= \frac{n_1 \cdot P_{\text{fut},1} + n_2 \cdot (P_{\text{cont}} - P_{\text{suc},2})}{1 - \text{Power}(\theta_C^*, \delta)}, \label{eq:seq_en_minus}
\end{align}
where $n_k = n_{T,k} + n_{C,k}$ is the total sample size at stage $k$, and $N^+$ (resp.\ $N^-$) denotes the number of patients enrolled in a trial that is declared successful (resp.\ unsuccessful).

\subsubsection{Average Operating Characteristics}
\label{subsubsec:avg_seq}

Following the averaging argument of Section~\ref{sec:pointwise}, we integrate the conditional metrics over the design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$:
\begin{align}
  \text{avgPower}(\delta) &= \int \text{Power}(\theta_C, \delta)\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C, \label{eq:seq_avgpower} \\
  \text{avgT1E}           &= \text{avgPower}(\delta = 0). \label{eq:seq_avgt1e}
\end{align}

The average expected sample sizes, conditional on trial outcome and hypothesis $H_i$ (with $\delta_i = 0$ under $H_0$ and $\delta_i = \delta_{\text{MCID}}$ under $H_1$), are weighted averages of the conditional expected sample sizes:
\begin{align}
  E_D[N^+ \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int E[N^+ \mid \theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot \text{Power}(\theta_C, \delta_i)\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{\text{avgPower}(\delta_i)}, \label{eq:seq_avg_enp} \\[6pt]
  E_D[N^- \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int E[N^- \mid \theta_C, \delta_i]\cdot (1-\text{Power}(\theta_C, \delta_i))\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{1-\text{avgPower}(\delta_i)}. \label{eq:seq_avg_enm}
\end{align}

Because the data-adaptive Bayesian boundary precludes closed-form evaluation, the integrals in Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_avgpower}--\eqref{eq:seq_avg_enm} are computed numerically.

\subsubsection{EUII for Sequential Designs}
\label{subsubsec:seq_euii}

In a sequential design, the sample size $N$ is random. A trial that stops early at stage 1 enrolls fewer patients than one that runs to the final analysis. \citet{held2025balancing} generalise the EUII to accommodate this by replacing the fixed exponent $1/n_{\text{trial}}$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:classical_euii} with outcome-specific effective information per patient:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:seq_euii}
  \text{EUII}_{\text{seq}} = \frac{(\text{LR}^+)^{E(1/N^+)}}{(\text{LR}^-)^{E(1/N^-)}},
\end{equation}
where $\text{LR}^+ = \text{avgPower}/\text{avgT1E}$ and $\text{LR}^- = (1-\text{avgPower})/(1-\text{avgT1E})$ are the likelihood ratios of a positive and a negative outcome respectively, computed using the average metrics of Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_avgpower}--\eqref{eq:seq_avgt1e}.

The exponents $E(1/N^+)$ and $E(1/N^-)$ are the expected inverse sample sizes, mixing across hypotheses according to their posterior probability given the trial outcome \citep{held2025balancing}:
\begin{align}
  E(1/N^+) &= E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_0]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{success}) + E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_1]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{success}), \label{eq:seq_einvnp} \\
  E(1/N^-) &= E_D[1/N^- \mid H_0]\cdot\Pr(H_0 \mid \text{no success}) + E_D[1/N^- \mid H_1]\cdot\Pr(H_1 \mid \text{no success}). \label{eq:seq_einvnm}
\end{align}

The hypothesis-specific expected inverse sample sizes are obtained by weighting the outcome-conditional inverse sample sizes by the probability of each outcome under $H_i$ and then normalising:
\begin{align}
  E_D[1/N^+ \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \frac{\text{Power}(\theta_C, \delta_i)}{E[N^+\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]}\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{\text{avgPower}(\delta_i)}, \label{eq:seq_inv_enp} \\[6pt]
  E_D[1/N^- \mid H_i] &= \frac{\displaystyle\int \frac{1-\text{Power}(\theta_C, \delta_i)}{E[N^-\mid\theta_C, \delta_i]}\, \pi_D(\theta_C)\, d\theta_C}{1-\text{avgPower}(\delta_i)}. \label{eq:seq_inv_enm}
\end{align}
The posterior probabilities $\Pr(H_0 \mid \cdot)$ and $\Pr(H_1 \mid \cdot)$ in Equations~\eqref{eq:seq_einvnp}--\eqref{eq:seq_einvnm} are determined by the prior probability $\Pr(H_1)$ and the DOR via Bayes' theorem.

When the design never stops early, $E[N^+ \mid \theta_C, \delta] = E[N^- \mid \theta_C, \delta] = n_{\text{trial}}$ for all $\theta_C$, so $E(1/N^+) = E(1/N^-) = 1/n_{\text{trial}}$ and Equation~\eqref{eq:seq_euii} reduces exactly to the fixed-design formula~\eqref{eq:euii_avg}. Under an effective sequential design, efficacy stopping at stage 1 concentrates $N^+$ at $n_1 < n_{\text{trial}}$, so $E(1/N^+) > 1/n_{\text{trial}}$; symmetrically, futility stopping raises $E(1/N^-)$ above $1/n_{\text{trial}}$. Both effects increase $\text{EUII}_{\text{seq}}$ relative to the fixed design, making sequential monitoring attractive not only for its patient-saving properties but also for the per-patient evidentiary yield it achieves.


