\subsection{Application 1: Borrowing on the Control Arm}

We evaluate the design of a Proof-of-Concept (PoC) study in Crohn’s disease, an inflammatory bowel disease. The primary endpoint is the change from baseline to week 6 in the Crohn’s Disease Activity Index (CDAI), a 
continuous measure where lower scores indicate clinical improvement.

The original trial evaluated the efficacy of secukinumab against a placebo \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}. 
Let $\theta_C$ and $\theta_T$ the true underlying mean changes for the control and treatment arms, respectively. 
The treatment effect is defined as the contrast $\delta = \theta_C - \theta_T$, where $\delta > 0$ represents a treatment benefit.


To improve trial efficiency, the original design derived an informative analysis prior ($\pi_A$) for the placebo arm using 671 
historical placebo patients from six previous Crohn’s disease trials via a Meta-Analytic-Predictive (MAP) approach.



\paragraph{Decision Rules}
At any given analysis, a successful terminal decision requires both strong evidence of a positive treatment effect and sufficient evidence of a clinically relevant 
effect size:
$$
\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.95 \quad \text{and} \quad \Pr(\delta > 50 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.50
$$

where $\mathcal{D}$ denotes the observed data up to that point. Furthermore, sequential designs in this application incorporate a Bayesian futility criterion. 
The trial can be stopped for futility if it is highly probable that the treatment effect is less than 40:

$$
\Pr(\delta < 40 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.90
 $$



\paragraph{Design Variations and Evaluation Strategy}
The literature presents varying implementations of this trial. The original protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab} planned two interim analyses, occurring when 17 and 41 patients, 
respectively, reached the 6 week evaluation time point. Conversely, \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} modified this into a two-stage group sequential design with a 1:2 allocation 
ratio (10 placebo and 20 experimental patients per stage). Taking a simpler approach, \citet{best_beyond_2025} evaluated the trial as a fixed design using 20 and 40 
individuals for the control and treatment arms, omitting the dual success criterion. Another cause of divergence is terminal decision: while \citet{gsponer_practical_2014} 
allowed a final "indeterminate" result by evaluating futility at the final analysis, we follow the original formulation, which restricts the final stage to a 
binary outcome (success or failure) by removing the terminal futility check.



Therefore, we will compute $\text{avgT1E}$, $\text{avgPower}$, and $\text{EUII}$ across three design variations of this trial:
 \begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Fixed Design:} A single-stage design (analogous to the evaluation by \citet{best_beyond_2025})  
  \item \textbf{One Interim Analysis:} An evaluation of the two-stage group-sequential design proposed by \citet{gsponer_practical_2014}.
  \item \textbf{Two Interim Analyses:} An evaluation of the original three-stage trial protocol \citep{hueber2012secukinumab}.

\end{enumerate}


Because the closed-form solutions derived in Section \ref{sec:pointwise} apply only to strictly Normal priors, we structure our evaluation in two phases. 
First, we compute the analytical operating characteristics assuming a strictly Normal analysis and design prior. Then, we transition 
to numerical evaluation, using a grid of analysis priors ($\pi_A$) and design priors ($\pi_D$) that are not strictly Normal, inspecting a sequence of true treatment 
effects $\delta$.




\paragraph{Analysis Priors ($\pi_A$)}
The analysis prior $\pi_A(\theta_C)$ determines the degree of historical information incorporated into the final posterior decision:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Normal MAP:} A single-component Normal prior obtained using the MAP approach on historical data to evaluate closed form results: $\pi_{\text{Normal}}(\theta_C) \sim \mathcal{N}(-49,\,\frac{88^2}{20})$: 
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A mixture of three Normals obtained using the MAP approach: 
  $\pi_{\text{MAP}}(\theta_C) = 0.51\,\mathcal{N}(-51,\,19.9^2) + 0.44\,\mathcal{N}(-46.8,\,7.6^2) + 0.05\,\mathcal{N}(-54.1,\,51.7^2)$
  \item \textbf{Robust MAP ($\omega \in \{0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8\}$):} Four robust mixture priors constructed by assigning a weight $\omega$ to a non-informative component and $(1-\omega)$ to the MAP 3-component prior. 
  The mean of the vague component is set to -49 to be consistent with the historical data, and its standard deviation is set to $\sigma = 88$, to be approximately equivalent to one subject's 
  worth of information. The value of $\omega$ reflects our prior belief about the possibility of non-exchangeability between the placebo effect from the historical studies and the 
  placebo effect in the new study.
  \item \textbf{Vague:} A non-informative prior representing a design with no historical borrowing, serving as the frequentist baseline: $\pi_{\text{vague}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-49,\,8800^2)$
\end{enumerate}




%% I am not using the advices of Weru since it is difficult to implement
\paragraph{Design Priors ($\pi_D$)}
The design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$ represents the assumed true data-generating process for the control arm ($\theta_C$):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Dirac:} A Dirac measure at $\theta_C^* = -49$, it recovers the classical conditional Type I Error and Power.
  \item \textbf{3-Component MAP:} A design prior exactly matching the corresponding 3-Component MAP analysis prior.
  \item \textbf{Skeptical:} Following the terminology of \citet{best_beyond_2025}, this prior corresponds to the posterior distribution of a stand-alone analysis of the historical study with the “most extreme” placebo effect: $\pi_{\text{skep}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-90,\,25^2)$. Because the placebo is exceptionally effective, this scenario is highly "skeptical" of the experimental treatment's ability to demonstrate superiority.
  \item \textbf{Misspecified:} A hypothetical prior-data conflict scenario where the true control arm performs much worse than historically predicted: $\pi_{\text{miss}}(\theta_C) = \mathcal{N}(-10,\,25^2)$.
\end{enumerate}




\subsubsection{Fixed Design}
Following \citep{best_beyond_2025}, we fix the sample size at $n_C = 20$ and $n_T = 40$ and analyze the single posterior decision rule $\Pr(\delta > 0 \mid \mathcal{D}, \pi_A) \ge 0.975$.

\paragraph{Normal priors case}
Using the MAP approach to obtain a Normal prior, the result is a prior mean of $\mu_{A,C} = -49$ and estimated prior effective sample size of $n_A = 20$, assuming a known patient-level standard deviation of $\sigma = 88$.
The prior variance is thus given by $\sigma_{A,C}^2 = \frac{88^2}{20}$.

Similarly, the sampling variances for the fixed design are $v_C = \frac{88^2}{20}$ and $v_T = \frac{88^2}{40}$. 
We can finally plug these values in the expression of avgPower derived in Equation~\eqref{eq:avgpower_closed_form}. 


Note that in the case analysis and design priors coincides, the Average Power simplifies to: 
\begin{align}
     \text{avgPower}(\delta) &= 1 - \Phi\left( \frac{0 + 1.96 \sqrt{V_{\delta}} - \big[0 + \delta\big]}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right) \nonumber \\
     &= 1 - \Phi\left( 1.96 - \frac{\delta}{\sqrt{V_{\delta}}} \right)
 \end{align}
Since, as proven in Appendix \ref{sec:appendix_normal_priors}, the bias term $(1 - W)(\mu_{A,C} - \mu_{D,C})$ vanishes entirely and the marginal variance in the denominator simplifies to exactly match the posterior variance $V_{\delta} = \frac{\sigma_{A,C}^2 v_C}{\sigma_{A,C}^2 + v_C} + v_T$. 

As already observed, when setting $\delta = 0$, the Average Type I Error with $\pi_A = \pi_D$ achieves the nominal $0.025$ level.

In Figures \ref{fig:plot_t1e}, \ref{fig:plot_power}, \ref{fig:plot_euii}, the analysis prior is held strictly constant ($\mu_{A,C} = -49, \sigma_{A,C}^2 = 88^2/20$) to represent a pre-specified trial design. We then inspect this design by varying the parameters of the design prior ($\pi_D$), representing the true, unknown data-generating process.


Figure \ref{fig:plot_t1e} visualizes how Type I Error reacts to two types of conflicts between analysis and design priors: location shift ($\mu_{A,C} \neq \mu_{D,C}$) and scale shift ($\sigma_{A,C} \neq \sigma_{D,C}$). The left panel shows that if the true placebo response is better than expected ($\mu_{D,C} < -49$), the Type I Error increases. Moreover, as expected, the conditional T1E (conditioned on $\theta_C^* = \mu_{D,C}$), is always below the avgT1E. 
In the right panel, when design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$ is zero, we obtain the conditional T1E, while as it approaches infinity (vague design prior), the Average Type I Error converges to $0.5$ (as observed analytically in Equation~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}).

<<plot_t1e, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Left panel shows Type I Error under Location Shift (varying $\\mu_{D,C}$), assuming $\\sigma_{D,C} = \\sigma_{A,C}$. Conditional metric is evaluated at $\\theta_C^* = \\mu_{D,C}$. Right panel shows Average Type I Error under Scale Shift (varying $\\sigma_{D,C}$) while setting $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(gridExtra)
library(scales)

# --- Trial Parameters & Functions  ---
sigma <- 88; n_C <- 20; n_T <- 40; n_A <- 20; N_total <- n_C + n_T
v_C <- sigma^2 / n_C; v_T <- sigma^2 / n_T; sigma_A2 <- sigma^2 / n_A; mu_A <- -49
q <- 0; z_p <- qnorm(0.975) 
W <- sigma_A2 / (sigma_A2 + v_C)
V_delta <- W * v_C + v_T
V_samp <- W^2 * v_C + v_T

condPower <- function(theta_C_star, delta) {
  num <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta) - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - theta_C_star) + delta)
  1 - pnorm(num / sqrt(V_samp))
}
avgPower <- function(mu_D, sigma_D2, delta) {
  num <- q + z_p * sqrt(V_delta) - ((1 - W) * (mu_A - mu_D) + delta)
  1 - pnorm(num / sqrt(V_samp + (1 - W)^2 * sigma_D2))
}
calc_EUII <- function(power, t1e, N) {
  eps <- 1e-10 
  p <- pmax(pmin(power, 1 - eps), eps); t <- pmax(pmin(t1e, 1 - eps), eps)

  log_dor <- qlogis(p) - qlogis(t) 
  return(exp(log_dor / N))
}

my_theme <- theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())
my_x_scale <- scale_x_continuous(trans = "log1p", breaks = c(0, 10, 100, 1000, 10000), labels = c("0\n(Cond.)", "10", "100", "1K", "10K"))

# --- T1E Data & Plots ---
df_t1e_loc <- data.frame(mu_D = seq(-90, -10, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional T1E` = condPower(mu_D, 0), 
    `Average T1E` = avgPower(mu_D, sigma_A2, 0)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional T1E`, `Average T1E`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

sigmas <- c(0, 10^seq(0, 5, length.out = 99))
df_t1e_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_T1E = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0))

p1a <- ggplot(df_t1e_loc, aes(x = mu_D, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1.2) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.025, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average T1E" = "#E69F00", "Conditional T1E" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * mu["D,C"] * ")"),
       y = "Type I Error") + 
  my_theme

p1b <- ggplot(df_t1e_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_T1E)) +
  geom_line(color = "#E69F00", size = 1.2) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale + 
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 0.6)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]), 
       y = "avgT1E") + 
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p1a, p1b, ncol = 2)
@

Figure \ref{fig:plot_power} shows the behavior of statistical power, assuming $\mu_{D,C} = \mu_{A,C} = -49$. In the left panel $\sigma_{D,C} = \sigma_{A,C}$ and we vary $\delta$. As already mentioned in \ref{sec:pointwise}, Average Power 
is always closer to $50\%$ compared to Conditional Power. 
The right panel shows how the avgPower at $\delta = 50$ changes as we increase the design uncertainty $\sigma_{D,C}$. When $\sigma_{D,C} = 0$, we obtain the conditional Power, while as $\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$, avgPower reaches 50\% (as observed analytically in Equation~\eqref{eq:avgt1e_closed_form}).

<<plot_power, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="Left panel shows Conditional and Average Statistical Power evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming perfectly aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows Average Power evaluated at $\\delta = 50$ as design uncertainty ($\\sigma_{D,C}$) increases, holding $\\mu_{D,C} = \\mu_{A,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- Power Data & Plots ---
df_pow_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    `Conditional Power` = condPower(mu_A, delta), 
    `Average Power` = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta)
  ) %>%
  pivot_longer(cols = c(`Conditional Power`, `Average Power`), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_pow_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_Power = avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 50))

p2a <- ggplot(df_pow_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) + 
  scale_color_manual(values = c("Average Power" = "#E69F00", "Conditional Power" = "#56B4E9")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), 
       y = "Power") + 
  my_theme

p2b <- ggplot(df_pow_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_Power)) +
  geom_line(color = "#56B4E9", size = 1.2) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.5, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
    geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale + 
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0.4, 1.0)) +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]), 
       y = "avgPower") + 
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p2a, p2b, ncol = 2)
@


In Figure \ref{fig:plot_euii}, EUII is plotted. The left panel shows that Average EUII provides a more conservative estimate of per-patient information than the Conditional EUII, 
the right panel shows that a completely vague design prior ($\sigma_{D,C} \to \infty$) drives the EUII to $1.0$.

<<plot_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.height=4, fig.width=10, fig.cap="Left panel shows Conditional and Average Experimental Unit Information Index (EUII) evaluated across true treatment effects ($\\delta$), assuming perfectly aligned priors ($\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$ and $\\sigma_{A,C} = \\sigma_{D,C}$). Right panel shows Average EUII evaluated at the clinical relevance threshold ($\\delta = 50$) as design uncertainty ($\\sigma_{D,C}$) increases, holding $\\mu_{A,C} = \\mu_{D,C}$. The vertical gray dotted lines indicate the points of prior alignment ($\\pi_D = \\pi_A$).">>=
# --- EUII Data & Plots ---
df_euii_loc <- data.frame(delta = seq(0, 100, length.out = 100)) %>%
  mutate(
    Cond_EUII = calc_EUII(condPower(mu_A, delta), condPower(mu_A, 0), N_total),
    Avg_EUII  = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, delta), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_A2, 0), N_total)
  ) %>% pivot_longer(cols = c(Cond_EUII, Avg_EUII), names_to = "Metric", values_to = "Value")

df_euii_scale <- data.frame(sigma_D = sigmas) %>%
  mutate(Avg_EUII = calc_EUII(avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 50), avgPower(mu_A, sigma_D^2, 0), N_total))

p3a <- ggplot(df_euii_loc, aes(x = delta, y = Value, color = Metric, linetype = Metric)) +
  geom_line(size = 1) + scale_color_manual(values = c("Avg_EUII" = "#009E73", "Cond_EUII" = "#CC79A7")) +
  labs(x = expression("True Effect (" * delta * ")"), y = "EUII") + my_theme

p3b <- ggplot(df_euii_scale, aes(x = sigma_D, y = Avg_EUII)) +
  geom_line(color = "#009E73", size = 1.2) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1.0, linetype = "dashed", color="black", linewidth = 0.6) +
    geom_vline(xintercept = sqrt(sigma_A2), linetype = "dotted", color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  my_x_scale +
  labs(x = expression(sigma["D,C"]), y = expression(EUII[avg])) + my_theme

grid.arrange(p3a, p3b, ncol = 2)
@



In summary, while the strictly Normal formulation yields closed-form solutions, it can be fragile in practice under prior misspecification.
 To achieve robustness, required for actual clinical application, we should transition to robust mixture priors.











\paragraph{Mixture priors} 

The Normal-prior closed-form results above serve as a tractable baseline, but MAP priors are usually mixtures of Normals, and robust extensions further add a vague component. Because no closed form exists for these cases, operating characteristics are computed numerically using \texttt{RBesT::oc2S} and a numerical integration routine for the averaged metrics.

\textbf{Conditional Operating Characteristics.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_cond} shows the conditional Type I Error ($\delta = 0$, left) and conditional Power at $\delta = 50$ (right) as functions of the fixed true control mean $\theta_C^*$.
The vague prior yields the flat frequentist baseline: Type I Error is exactly $0.025$ everywhere and power is determined solely by the sample size. As historical borrowing increases (decreasing $\omega$), Type I Error inflates with increasing severity when $\theta_C^* \ll -49$ (skeptical scenario) and is increasingly suppressed well below $\alpha$ when $\theta_C^* \gg -49$ (misspecified scenario). The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\mu_{A,C} = -49$. Robust MAP priors exhibit a monotone moderation: 
as $\omega$ increases, the curve is pulled toward the flat vague baseline, capping the T1E at the cost of reduced Power. 

<<plot_mix_cond, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Conditional metrics for all six analysis priors as a function of the fixed true control mean $\\theta_C^*$. Left: conditional Type I Error ($\\delta = 0$). Right: conditional Power at $\\delta = 50$. The Vague prior (dashed) provides the frequentist reference. The dotted vertical line marks the prior mean $\\mu_{A,C} = -49$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_cond   <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_cond.RDS")
df_cond    <- res_cond$df_cond
prior_names <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                 "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
df_cond$Prior <- factor(df_cond$Prior, levels = prior_names)
mu_A <- -49

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_cond_T1E <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = T1E, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.025, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = "Conditional Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_cond_Power <- ggplot(df_cond,
    aes(x = theta_C, y = Power, color = Prior, linetype = Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mu_A, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "gray", linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("True Control Mean (" * theta[C]^"*" * ")"),
       y = bquote("Conditional Power (" * delta * " = 50)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_cond_T1E, p_cond_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{Average Metrics.}
By marginalizing the conditional curves over a design prior $\pi_D(\theta_C)$, we obtain the Average Type I Error (avgT1E) and Average Power for each of the 24 analysis$\times$design prior combinations.
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_avg} displays on the $x$-axis the design prior (representing different assumptions about the true data-generating process) and each line as a given analysis prior.
When the analysis and design prior are aligned (MAP analysis, MAP design), avgT1E is at the nominal $0.025$, consistent with the theoretical property. Under the skeptical design prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -90$), more informative analysis priors incur into larger avgT1E inflation. The misspecified prior ($\mu_{D,C} = -10$) drives the opposite effect: more informative prior overcorrects the control mean downward, suppressing both avgT1E and Average Power simultaneously, making the trial conservative but underpowered.
Note that the Dirac design prior ($\pi_D = \delta_{\{-49\}}$) concentrates all mass at the prior mean, so the averaged metrics reduce exactly to the conditional counterparts evaluated at $\theta_C^* = -49$; it serves as the best-case reference where the truth is deterministic.


<<plot_mix_avg, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=10, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="Average Type I Error (left) and Average Power at $\\delta = 50$ (right) for all combinations of analysis prior and design prior. Each line connects values for a single analysis prior across the four design prior scenarios. The dashed horizontal line marks the nominal $\\alpha = 0.025$.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)
library(gridExtra)

res_avg      <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−49)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_avg <- res_avg$df_euii |>
  dplyr::filter(delta == 50) |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

p_avg_T1E <- ggplot(df_avg,
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgT1E,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0.025, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior", y = "Average Type I Error") +
  my_theme

p_avg_Power <- ggplot(df_avg,
    aes(x = Design_Prior, y = avgPower,
        color = Analysis_Prior, group = Analysis_Prior,
        linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.0) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = "Design Prior",
       y = bquote("Average Power (" * delta * " = 50)")) +
  my_theme

grid.arrange(p_avg_T1E, p_avg_Power, ncol = 2)
@


\textbf{EUII.}
Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii} plots the EUII at $\delta = 50$ against the robustness weight $\omega$, with one curve per design prior. The dotted horizontal line marks the vague prior baseline ($\omega = 1$). Reading the four curves together can constitute a sensitivity analysis over the data-generating process: for any proposed $\omega$, the investigator can read off the expected per-patient efficiency under each scenario simultaneously. Under the aligned design prior (MAP), EUII is strictly decreasing in $\omega$ and always above the baseline, since more borrowing is better when the prior is correct. Under the skeptical design prior, all informative analysis priors fall below the vague baseline and $\omega = 1$ (no borrowing) is optimal. 
Under the misspecified design prior, EUII also lies above the baseline for all $\omega$, but this is misleading: the prior suppresses avgT1E so aggressively that the DOR is inflated despite average Power dropping to around $36\%$ at $\delta = 50$. In this context, EUII should always be read alongside average Power. The choice of $\omega$ thus reflects the investigator's belief about which design prior is most plausible: the more confident one is in prior alignment, the smaller the $\omega$ that can be justified; the more one fears prior-data conflict, the larger $\omega$ must be.

<<plot_mix_euii, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=8, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII at $\\delta = 50$ as a function of the robustness weight $\\omega$ for each design prior. The dotted horizontal line marks the vague-prior baseline ($\\omega = 1$). Under aligned priors, EUII is maximized at full borrowing ($\\omega = 0$); under the skeptical design prior it collapses below the baseline for informative analysis priors.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−49)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

df_euii_50 <- dplyr::filter(df_euii, delta == 50)
euii_vague <- dplyr::filter(df_euii_50, Analysis_Prior == "Vague") |>
  dplyr::pull(EUII) |> unique()

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii_50,
       aes(x = omega, y = EUII,
           color = Design_Prior, group = Design_Prior)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  geom_point(size = 2.5) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = euii_vague, linetype = "dotted",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(0, 1, 0.2)) +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression("Robustness weight (" * omega * ")"),
       y = expression("EUII (" * delta * " = 50)")) +
  my_theme
@

Figure~\ref{fig:plot_mix_euii_delta} shows the EUII as a function of $\delta$ for all 24 analysis-prior/design-prior combinations. Under the aligned design 
prior (MAP), EUII rises monotonically and the ordering is strict: more borrowing (smaller $\omega$) always dominates, confirming that historical information strictly increases EUII when the prior is correctly specified. 
The Dirac design prior, which fixes the truth at the prior mean, acts as the ceiling of efficiency gains.

<<plot_mix_euii_delta, echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.width=12, dev='cairo_pdf', fig.cap="EUII as a function of the treatment effect $\\delta$, faceted by design prior. Color encodes the analysis prior (Vague shown dashed). The dashed horizontal line marks $\\text{EUII} = 1$, the no-information baseline.">>=
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

res_euii     <- readRDS("../Output/FIXED_euii.RDS")
prior_names  <- c("MAP", "Robust (w=0.2)", "Robust (w=0.4)",
                  "Robust (w=0.6)", "Robust (w=0.8)", "Vague")
dprior_names <- c("Dirac (−49)", "MAP", "Skeptical (−90)", "Misspecified (−10)")
df_euii <- res_euii$df_euii |>
  dplyr::mutate(
    Analysis_Prior = factor(Analysis_Prior, levels = prior_names),
    Design_Prior   = factor(Design_Prior,   levels = dprior_names)
  )

my_theme <- theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.title = element_blank())

ggplot(df_euii,
       aes(x = delta, y = EUII,
           color = Analysis_Prior,
           linetype = Analysis_Prior == "Vague")) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.2) +
  facet_wrap(~Design_Prior, nrow = 1) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed",
             color = "black", linewidth = 0.6) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("TRUE" = "dashed", "FALSE" = "solid"),
                        guide = "none") +
  scale_color_viridis_d(option = "turbo", direction = -1) +
  labs(x = expression(delta), y = "EUII") +
  my_theme
@


